title: pidly set and pidly fields: writing a field, and filling slots after a schema change
effort: M
area: cli
created: 2026-09-17T06:16:28Z
updated: 2026-09-17T06:16:28Z

Nothing in the CLI sets a field. release -m writes handoff and that is the
whole list; set_field exists inside the script and is not exposed. So a
per-ticket value is pidly edit and your editor, and a bulk change is an awk
loop over every info file. Adding a row to schema.md leaves every existing
ticket without the slot, which is the gap this closes.

  pidly set <n> <field> [value]    one ticket; no value clears it
  pidly fields                     add missing declared keys to every ticket

set replaces or appends within the field block, under the lock, the way
release already writes handoff. It refuses a field the schema does not
declare — that is what check would flag a second later.

fields is the migration. It adds keys a ticket is missing, in schema order,
and does nothing else: it never removes a field the schema dropped, because
that is data, and never reorders existing lines, because nothing may depend
on position (see 24). Explicit, never part of init — init is what you run
after an upgrade, and it must not quietly rewrite every ticket in the
tracker. check grows a report for the drift so there is a way to see what
fields would do before running it.

It must not bump updated. It is pidly writing, but it is not activity on the
work, and a migration that touched every ticket would flatten list --stale
to nothing. Same reasoning that keeps close and reopen from bumping it.

Depends on 24 for the syntax: -f k=v and set <n> <field> <value> have to
agree about what a value is, and about whether the values column in
schema.md is validated or is documentation.

Open question: whether fields is worth a --dry-run of its own, or whether
check reporting the drift is enough.

Done when both commands exist, a schema row added after the fact can be
rolled out across a tracker in one command, updated is untouched by it, and
the tests cover a field added mid-life, a ticket that already has it, a
ticket made by hand with fields in a different order, and a closed ticket.
